## Rural areas have a higher weighted mean supply (0.056) than urban (0.025)
Seems counterintuitive but has a plausible explanation — rural Head Start programs are often federally funded at higher per-child rates because they serve harder-to-reach populations, so where they exist, they may serve a larger share of the local eligible population
However, the rural median is 0, meaning the typical rural area has absolutely no Head Start supply at all, suggesting rural Head Start is highly concentrated in a few well-served communities rather than broadly distributed
Urban areas, by contrast, have a slightly higher median (0.003) — meaning more urban areas have some supply, but it's spread thin across a much larger eligible population, keeping the per-child ratio low.
Urban deserts: nearly everyone is underserved, but most areas have at least a token amount of supply
Rural deserts: most areas have zero supply, but the few that do have it tend to have relatively more of it

## Next, broke down the share of areas with some supply by rurality
childcare_with_poverty %>%
filter(!is.na(adj_supply_hs), !is.na(rural)) %>%
mutate(
supply_category = case_when(
adj_supply_hs == 0              ~ "No supply",
adj_supply_hs <= desert_threshold ~ "Some supply, still a desert",
TRUE                            ~ "Not a desert"
)
) %>%
group_by(rural, supply_category) %>%
summarise(n = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
group_by(rural) %>%
mutate(pct = round(n / sum(n) * 100, 1)) %>%
arrange(rural, supply_category)

