Urban areas (rural = 0):
Most areas (79.1%) have some Head Start supply but not nearly enough
Only 20.7% have zero supply entirely
A mere 0.2% are adequately served

Rural areas (rural = 1):
Nearly half (48.6%) have zero Head Start supply whatsoever — a much starker form of deprivation than urban areas
The other half (48.7%) have some supply but remain deserts
Only 2.7% are adequately served, though this is still notably higher than urban (0.2%), consistent with the higher weighted mean from above

Could mean qualitatively different problems with Head Start access by rurality:
Urban areas suffer from diluted supply — programs exist but are overwhelmed by the sheer volume of eligible children
Rural areas suffer from absent supply — nearly half have no program at all, which is an access cliff rather than a gap
